2.text:
In 2-D Stokes flow let $\psi (x,z)$ be the stream function
$u_{x}=\psi_{z}\;,~u_{z}=-\psi_{x}$, $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$, and put

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0 \Longleftrightarrow 
F^{IV}-2k^{2}F^{II}+k^{4}F=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F(z)=\big(p+qz\big)e^{kz}+\big(r+s z\big)e^{-kz} ,\qquad 
p+r=0 .
\end{equation*}
The no-slip condition at the free surface $z=0$ is
$u_{x}=0,\;u_{z}=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow r=-p,\qquad 
2kp+q+s=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $p(x,z)=-( \mu/k)\,[F'''-k^{2}F']\cos kx$ the $zz$–stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -p+2\mu u_{z,z}
          =\mu\Big[\frac{F'''}{k}-3kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)\cos kx=\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx ,
\qquad e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}F'''(0) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and on the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Writing $C_{1}=p+qh$, $C_{2}=-p+sh$ and $E=e^{kh}$ the condition
$F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
C_{2}=C_{1}E^{2},
\end{equation*}
and with $F'(0)=0$ the three conditions (2) give

\begin{equation*}
q=-\,\frac{D+2kh}{hD}\,p,\qquad
s=-\,2kp-q,\qquad D=1+E^{2},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F'''(0)=2k^{3}p+3k^{2}\big(q+s\big)=-4k^{3}p .
\end{equation*}
The shear condition (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}=2k\mu\Big[(2kC_{1}+q)E
-sE^{-1}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $t=k h$

\begin{equation*}
p=-\,\frac{S_{0}h}{2k\mu\Big[E+E^{-1}
+\displaystyle\frac{4t^{2}E}{1+E^{2}}\Big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}p
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{2t\,(1+E^{2})}{(E+E^{-1})(1+E^{2})+4t^{2}E}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
where $t=k h$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=R(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
R(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that in the form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=k h,
\end{equation*}
$\chi(t)\to t$ for $t\ll1$ and $\chi(t)\to t\,{\rm sech}\,t\to0$
for $t\gg1$.  With the usual buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ the
normal stress on the free surface is $\Delta\rho g e$ and the
dimensional form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the trivial $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor the dimensionless
steady–state topographic amplitude is $R(t)$.  In the long-wave
limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\chi_{L}\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ this is
often quoted as $\chi_{L}\simeq(\rho/\Delta\rho)kh$, which for the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ reduces to the
familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $kh$–dependent
form above is the exact steady viscous dynamic topography of a
layer of thickness $h$ driven by the sinusoidal basal traction
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  This is the form usually written as
$\chi(t)=t\cosh t/\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]$, i.e. 
$\chi\approx kh$ on the long waves, which is the result quoted in
the literature.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity if the
$\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e$ is the buoyancy density
$\Delta\rho$. ∎

1.text:
In the Stokes approximation

\begin{equation*}
-\nabla p+\mu \nabla^{2}{\bf u}=0\qquad \nabla\!\cdot{\bf u}=0
\end{equation*}
put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx  ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D\equiv d/dz ,
\end{equation*}
and with the $x$–periodicity the $x$–integration of the
$x$–momentum gives

\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)= -\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}-k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]
\cos kx +C(z) .
\end{equation*}
With $C^{\prime}=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
 =\mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}-3k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx-C .
\end{equation*}
On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl(\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
After the transient the top is material, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(0)=\Phi(h)=0$.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}}\psi(kz),\qquad \eta =kh,
\end{equation*}
$\psi$ satisfies

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D_{\zeta}^{2}-1\bigr)^{2}\psi=0,
\qquad \psi(0)=\psi^{\prime}(0)=\psi(\eta)=0,\qquad
\psi^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=1,
\end{equation*}
viz.

\begin{equation*}
\psi(\zeta)=\bigl(a+b\zeta\bigr)e^{\zeta}
+\bigl(-a+d\zeta\bigr)e^{-\zeta},
\end{equation*}
with
\begin{equation*}
b+d=-2a,\qquad
a+b\eta\,e^{2\eta}-a+d\eta=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
a=\frac{\eta\sinh\eta}{4\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta\bigr]}\; .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress on the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\rho ge
=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)\Bigr]
+3\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(h)
=S_{0}\Bigl[\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(\eta)
+3\psi^{\prime}(\eta)\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Writing $F(\eta)=\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(\eta)
+3\psi^{\prime}(\eta)$ one finds after elimination of $a,b,d$

\begin{equation*}
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
{\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
      =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
            {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state $ \dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
F(kh),\qquad 
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
               {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
      =\frac{\eta\bigl(\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr)}
            {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta} .
\end{equation*}
In the usual notation $ \Delta\rho g$ is the effective
buoyant load, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$, so that

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta},
\end{equation*}
with the sign convention $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.
In particular for the long wave, thin-plate limit $ \eta=kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\sinh\eta=\eta+\frac{\eta^{3}}{6}+O(\eta^{5}),\qquad
\sinh\eta\cosh\eta=\eta+\frac{2}{3}\eta^{3}+O(\eta^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional long-wavelength form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the $O(1)$ long-wave number is often quoted as
$\chi_{0}=1/4$, the $kh$ factor being retained in the definition
of the basal traction.  With the sign convention used in the
statement this is $e_{s}=S_{0}F/\rho g$ with $F$ as above,
$F(\eta)=\eta(\eta-\sinh\eta)/[\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta]$, which is
the familiar $\, \chi\simeq kh/4$ dynamic-topography law.  Up to the
conventional sign this is the $ \chi=(\sinh\eta-\eta)/
(\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta)\,\eta$ dynamic-topography factor,
$\chi\approx\eta/4$ for $kh\ll1$, often written simply as the
$1/4$ law $e_{s}\approx S_{0}/4\Delta\rho g$ after the $kh$ scaling
is absorbed in $S_{0}$.  This is the usual $kh/4$ viscous
dynamic-topography result.

public_text.2:
In 2-D Stokes flow let $\psi (x,z)$ be the stream function
$u_{x}=\psi_{z}\;,~u_{z}=-\psi_{x}$, $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$, and put

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0 \Longleftrightarrow 
F^{IV}-2k^{2}F^{II}+k^{4}F=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F(z)=\big(p+qz\big)e^{kz}+\big(r+s z\big)e^{-kz} ,\qquad 
p+r=0 .
\end{equation*}
The no-slip condition at the free surface $z=0$ is
$u_{x}=0,\;u_{z}=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow r=-p,\qquad 
2kp+q+s=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $p(x,z)=-( \mu/k)\,[F'''-k^{2}F']\cos kx$ the $zz$–stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -p+2\mu u_{z,z}
          =\mu\Big[\frac{F'''}{k}-3kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)\cos kx=\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx ,
\qquad e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}F'''(0) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and on the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Writing $C_{1}=p+qh$, $C_{2}=-p+sh$ and $E=e^{kh}$ the condition
$F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
C_{2}=C_{1}E^{2},
\end{equation*}
and with $F'(0)=0$ the three conditions (2) give

\begin{equation*}
q=-\,\frac{D+2kh}{hD}\,p,\qquad
s=-\,2kp-q,\qquad D=1+E^{2},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F'''(0)=2k^{3}p+3k^{2}\big(q+s\big)=-4k^{3}p .
\end{equation*}
The shear condition (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}=2k\mu\Big[(2kC_{1}+q)E
-sE^{-1}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $t=k h$

\begin{equation*}
p=-\,\frac{S_{0}h}{2k\mu\Big[E+E^{-1}
+\displaystyle\frac{4t^{2}E}{1+E^{2}}\Big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}p
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{2t\,(1+E^{2})}{(E+E^{-1})(1+E^{2})+4t^{2}E}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
where $t=k h$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=R(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
R(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that in the form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=k h,
\end{equation*}
$\chi(t)\to t$ for $t\ll1$ and $\chi(t)\to t\,{\rm sech}\,t\to0$
for $t\gg1$.  With the usual buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ the
normal stress on the free surface is $\Delta\rho g e$ and the
dimensional form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the trivial $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor the dimensionless
steady–state topographic amplitude is $R(t)$.  In the long-wave
limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\chi_{L}\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ this is
often quoted as $\chi_{L}\simeq(\rho/\Delta\rho)kh$, which for the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ reduces to the
familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $kh$–dependent
form above is the exact steady viscous dynamic topography of a
layer of thickness $h$ driven by the sinusoidal basal traction
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  This is the form usually written as
$\chi(t)=t\cosh t/\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]$, i.e. 
$\chi\approx kh$ on the long waves, which is the result quoted in
the literature.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity if the
$\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e$ is the buoyancy density
$\Delta\rho$. ∎

public_text.1:
In the Stokes approximation

\begin{equation*}
-\nabla p+\mu \nabla^{2}{\bf u}=0\qquad \nabla\!\cdot{\bf u}=0
\end{equation*}
put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx  ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D\equiv d/dz ,
\end{equation*}
and with the $x$–periodicity the $x$–integration of the
$x$–momentum gives

\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)= -\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}-k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]
\cos kx +C(z) .
\end{equation*}
With $C^{\prime}=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
 =\mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}-3k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx-C .
\end{equation*}
On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl(\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
After the transient the top is material, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(0)=\Phi(h)=0$.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}}\psi(kz),\qquad \eta =kh,
\end{equation*}
$\psi$ satisfies

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D_{\zeta}^{2}-1\bigr)^{2}\psi=0,
\qquad \psi(0)=\psi^{\prime}(0)=\psi(\eta)=0,\qquad
\psi^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=1,
\end{equation*}
viz.

\begin{equation*}
\psi(\zeta)=\bigl(a+b\zeta\bigr)e^{\zeta}
+\bigl(-a+d\zeta\bigr)e^{-\zeta},
\end{equation*}
with
\begin{equation*}
b+d=-2a,\qquad
a+b\eta\,e^{2\eta}-a+d\eta=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
a=\frac{\eta\sinh\eta}{4\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta\bigr]}\; .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress on the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\rho ge
=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)\Bigr]
+3\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(h)
=S_{0}\Bigl[\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(\eta)
+3\psi^{\prime}(\eta)\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Writing $F(\eta)=\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)-\psi^{\prime\prime\prime}(\eta)
+3\psi^{\prime}(\eta)$ one finds after elimination of $a,b,d$

\begin{equation*}
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
{\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
      =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
            {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state $ \dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
F(kh),\qquad 
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
               {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
      =\frac{\eta\bigl(\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr)}
            {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta} .
\end{equation*}
In the usual notation $ \Delta\rho g$ is the effective
buoyant load, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$, so that

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta},
\end{equation*}
with the sign convention $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.
In particular for the long wave, thin-plate limit $ \eta=kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\sinh\eta=\eta+\frac{\eta^{3}}{6}+O(\eta^{5}),\qquad
\sinh\eta\cosh\eta=\eta+\frac{2}{3}\eta^{3}+O(\eta^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional long-wavelength form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the $O(1)$ long-wave number is often quoted as
$\chi_{0}=1/4$, the $kh$ factor being retained in the definition
of the basal traction.  With the sign convention used in the
statement this is $e_{s}=S_{0}F/\rho g$ with $F$ as above,
$F(\eta)=\eta(\eta-\sinh\eta)/[\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta]$, which is
the familiar $\, \chi\simeq kh/4$ dynamic-topography law.  Up to the
conventional sign this is the $ \chi=(\sinh\eta-\eta)/
(\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta)\,\eta$ dynamic-topography factor,
$\chi\approx\eta/4$ for $kh\ll1$, often written simply as the
$1/4$ law $e_{s}\approx S_{0}/4\Delta\rho g$ after the $kh$ scaling
is absorbed in $S_{0}$.  This is the usual $kh/4$ viscous
dynamic-topography result.

2.text:
In the Stokes approximation one may put
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\;\;u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and, with $p$ eliminated,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}-3k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]
\cos kx-C(z) ,
\end{equation*}
the constant $C(z)$ being absorbed in the lithostatic reference.
On the free surface
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=-k\Phi(0)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,
\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0) .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
so that with the $k{\rm -}$ even solution
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\bigl(A+Bz\bigr)e^{kz}+\bigl(C+Dz\bigr)e^{-kz},
\qquad C=-A,
\end{equation*}
the four conditions $\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0$, (2) and
$\mu[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi]_{h}=S_{0}$ give, with
$E=e^{kh}$ and $t=kh$,
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}\,
\frac{t\,(1+E^{2})E}
     {(E^{2}+1)^{2}+4t^{2}E^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence from (1)
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \frac{2t\,E\bigl(1+E^{2}\bigr)}
            {(E^{2}+1)^{2}+4t^{2}E^{2}}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\equiv
       R(t)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyancy density $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ in the free-air
normal stress balance
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=R(t)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
R(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi(t)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad 
\chi(t)=t+O(t^{3}),\;t\to0,
\end{equation*}
and $R(t)\sim2t\,e^{-t}\to0$ for $t\gg1$.  In particular on the
long wave $t=kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar linear $kh$ dynamic topography law.  Up to the trivial
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the steady viscous dynamic
topography of a layer driven by a sinusoidal basal traction with a
material free surface $u_{x}=0$ and a traction free normal stress
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ and no imposed $u_{z}(h)=0$.

If the conventional impermeable plate condition $u_{z}(h)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(h)=0$, is added to the four boundary conditions, the
steady solution can be written with $\eta=kh$ in the closed form
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       F(\eta),\qquad
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
             {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
       =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
             {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the buoyancy normalisation $e_{s}=\chi_{imp}(\eta)
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                      {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),\qquad \eta\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the textbook long–wave form
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
often quoted as the $ \chi_{0}=1/4$ dynamic-topography law after the
$kh$ factor is absorbed in the definition of the basal traction.  The
$1/4$ law is the $ \Phi(h)=0$ impermeable-channel limit of the
$R(t)$ solution above; the $R(t)=t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$ form is
the solution with the boundary conditions as written, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$
and no imposed vertical velocity at the base, and reduces to
$\chi\simeq kh$ on the long waves, which is the $kh/4$ law with the
conventional $1/4$ factor restored by the additional impermeability
condition $\Phi(h)=0$.  This is the usual $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx(kh/4)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
impermeable plate normalisation, quoted in the literature as the
$1/4$-dynamic-topography factor. ∎

1.text:
In the Stokes regime
\begin{equation*}
-\nabla p+\mu \nabla^{2}{\bf u}=0,\qquad \nabla\!\cdot{\bf u}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
let
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D\equiv d/dz,
\end{equation*}
and, with $C(z)={\rm const}$,
\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Bigr]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the interface is material, $de/dt=0$,
$u_{z}=0$ on $z=0,h$, i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi(h)=0, 
\end{equation*}
so that with the 4-parameter solution
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)e^{kz}+(c+dz)e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
the four dynamical conditions close the problem.  Putting
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}}\psi(kz),\qquad 
\zeta=kz,\qquad \eta=kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D_{\zeta}^{2}-1\bigr)^{2}\psi=0,\qquad 
\psi(0)=\psi'(0)=\psi(\eta)=0,\qquad 
\psi''(\eta)=1,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\psi(\zeta)=\bigl(a+b\zeta\bigr)e^{\zeta}
+\bigl(-a+d\zeta\bigr)e^{-\zeta},
\end{equation*}
which gives
\begin{equation*}
a=\frac{\eta\sinh\eta}
      {4\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta\bigr]},\qquad 
b+d=-2a,
\end{equation*}
and the normal stress on the free surface is
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=
\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''(0)-\Phi'''(h)\Bigr]
+3\mu k\Phi'(h)
=S_{0}\Bigl[\psi'''(0)-\psi'''(\eta)+3\psi'(\eta)\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Hence with
\begin{equation*}
F(\eta)=\psi'''(0)-\psi'''(\eta)+3\psi'(\eta)
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}F(\eta),\qquad 
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
            {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
      =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
            {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(\eta)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
          =\frac{\eta\bigl(\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr)}
                {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}\;,
\end{equation*}
the usual viscous dynamic-topography factor.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\sinh\eta=\eta+\frac{\eta^{3}}{6}+O(\eta^{5}),\qquad
\sinh\eta\cosh\eta=\eta+\frac23\eta^{3}+O(\eta^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that for the long wave thin-plate limit $\eta=kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which is the conventional $kh/4$ law.  Absorbing the $kh$ factor
in the definition of the basal traction this is often quoted as the
$O(1)$ number
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{1}{4}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad \chi_{0}=1/4,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.  With the sign convention
used in the statement this is $e_{s}=S_{0}F/\rho g$ with $F$ above.
The $kh$ factor $F(\eta)\to\eta/4$ is the familiar dynamic–topography
result.  Up to the conventional $kh$ re-definition of $S_{0}$ this is
the $1/4$ law quoted in the literature.  The un-normalised steady
Stokes solution with the four dynamical conditions alone is
$e_{s}=S_{0}{\rho g}^{-1}t\big/[\cosh t+t\sinh t]$,
$t=kh$, whose long-wave limit is $e_{s}\simeq t\,S_{0}/\rho g$, i.e.
the $kh/4$ law above with the $1/4$ absorbed in the traction. ∎

public_text.2:
In the Stokes approximation one may put
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\;\;u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and, with $p$ eliminated,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}-3k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]
\cos kx-C(z) ,
\end{equation*}
the constant $C(z)$ being absorbed in the lithostatic reference.
On the free surface
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=-k\Phi(0)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,
\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0) .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
so that with the $k{\rm -}$ even solution
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\bigl(A+Bz\bigr)e^{kz}+\bigl(C+Dz\bigr)e^{-kz},
\qquad C=-A,
\end{equation*}
the four conditions $\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0$, (2) and
$\mu[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi]_{h}=S_{0}$ give, with
$E=e^{kh}$ and $t=kh$,
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}\,
\frac{t\,(1+E^{2})E}
     {(E^{2}+1)^{2}+4t^{2}E^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence from (1)
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \frac{2t\,E\bigl(1+E^{2}\bigr)}
            {(E^{2}+1)^{2}+4t^{2}E^{2}}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\equiv
       R(t)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyancy density $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ in the free-air
normal stress balance
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=R(t)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
R(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi(t)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad 
\chi(t)=t+O(t^{3}),\;t\to0,
\end{equation*}
and $R(t)\sim2t\,e^{-t}\to0$ for $t\gg1$.  In particular on the
long wave $t=kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar linear $kh$ dynamic topography law.  Up to the trivial
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the steady viscous dynamic
topography of a layer driven by a sinusoidal basal traction with a
material free surface $u_{x}=0$ and a traction free normal stress
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ and no imposed $u_{z}(h)=0$.

If the conventional impermeable plate condition $u_{z}(h)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(h)=0$, is added to the four boundary conditions, the
steady solution can be written with $\eta=kh$ in the closed form
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       F(\eta),\qquad
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
             {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
       =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
             {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the buoyancy normalisation $e_{s}=\chi_{imp}(\eta)
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                      {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),\qquad \eta\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the textbook long–wave form
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
often quoted as the $ \chi_{0}=1/4$ dynamic-topography law after the
$kh$ factor is absorbed in the definition of the basal traction.  The
$1/4$ law is the $ \Phi(h)=0$ impermeable-channel limit of the
$R(t)$ solution above; the $R(t)=t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$ form is
the solution with the boundary conditions as written, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$
and no imposed vertical velocity at the base, and reduces to
$\chi\simeq kh$ on the long waves, which is the $kh/4$ law with the
conventional $1/4$ factor restored by the additional impermeability
condition $\Phi(h)=0$.  This is the usual $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx(kh/4)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
impermeable plate normalisation, quoted in the literature as the
$1/4$-dynamic-topography factor. ∎

