public_text.1:
In Stokes flow with the stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\;\;u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
 \bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
 p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi'''}{k}-3k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
on the material free surface $z=0$
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=-k\Phi(0)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0 ,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
For an incompressible material plate the normal velocity vanishes on
both bounding surfaces in the steady state,
\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=u_{z}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
\Phi(0)=\Phi(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Phi(z)=\mu^{-1}k^{-2}S_{0}\psi(kz)$,
$\zeta=kz$, $\eta=kh$,

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D_{\zeta}^{2}-1\bigr)^{2}\psi=0,\qquad 
\psi(0)=\psi'(0)=\psi(\eta)=0,\qquad\psi''(\eta)=1 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
\psi(\zeta)=\bigl(a+b\zeta\bigr)e^{\zeta}
+\bigl(-a+d\zeta\bigr)e^{-\zeta},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\Big[\psi'''(0)-\psi'''(\eta)+3\psi'(\eta)\Big]
\equiv S_{0}F(\eta) ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
             {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
       =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
             {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0 .
\end{equation*}
With the buoyancy normal stress $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(\eta)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\eta)=F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                     {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta},
\end{equation*}
the impermeable--channel dynamic topography factor.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\sinh\eta=\eta+\frac{\eta^{3}}{6}+O(\eta^{5}),\qquad
\sinh\eta\cosh\eta=\eta+\frac{2}{3}\eta^{3}+O(\eta^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that for the long wave thin plate limit $\eta=kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}>0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the $kh$ factor absorbed in the definition of the basal
traction the conventional $O(1)$ dynamic topography law

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}=\frac14,
\end{equation*}
is recovered.  This is the familiar $e_{s}\approx S_{0}/4\Delta\rho g$
sign convention $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.  The four dynamical
conditions alone give the un-normalised form
$e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}t/[\cosh t+t\sinh t]$,
$t=kh$, whose long wave limit is $e_{s}\simeq tS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the $kh/4$ law above with the conventional $1/4$ absorbed in the
traction definition.  The $R(t)=t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$ form is the
$\Phi(h)\ne0$ solution with the four dynamical conditions only and
reduces to $\chi_{imp}$ on imposing the impermeability $\Phi(h)=0$.
∎

public_text.2:
In the Stokes approximation the stream function can be written

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$,

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $u_{x}(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =\Delta\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k\Delta\rho g}\Phi'''(0) .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
With
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}}\,\psi(\zeta),\qquad 
\zeta=kz,\qquad \eta=kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D_{\zeta}^{2}-1\bigr)^{2}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
and the free surface conditions become
\begin{equation*}
\psi(0)=\psi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear is
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow\psi''(\eta)=1 .
\end{equation*}
With the conventional impermeable plate condition
$u_{z}(h)=0$, i.e. $\Phi(h)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\psi(\eta)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
the three homogeneous conditions determine $\psi$ up to the
overall scale set by $\psi''(\eta)=1$.  Writing
\begin{equation*}
\psi(\zeta)=\bigl(a+b\zeta\bigr)e^{\zeta}
          +\bigl(-a+d\zeta\bigr)e^{-\zeta},
\end{equation*}
$\psi(0)=\psi'(0)=0$ gives $b+d=-2a$ and the remaining two
conditions give

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{1}{2\bigl[\sinh\eta+\eta\cosh\eta\bigr]},\qquad 
b+d=-2a,\qquad b=d ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the normalisation
\begin{equation*}
\psi'''(0)=\frac{2}{\sinh\eta+\eta\cosh\eta}\,
          \frac{\sinh\eta-\eta}{\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta} .
\end{equation*}
Hence from (1)
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      F(\eta),\qquad 
F(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl[\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr]}
               {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta}
       =\frac{\eta\bigl[\eta-\sinh\eta\bigr]}
              {\eta-\sinh\eta\cosh\eta}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual buoyancy normalisation
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{{\rm imp}}(\eta)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}, \qquad
\chi_{{\rm imp}}(\eta)=\frac{\eta\bigl(\sinh\eta-\eta\bigr)}
                           {\sinh\eta\cosh\eta-\eta} .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\sinh\eta=\eta+\frac{\eta^{3}}6+O(\eta^{5}),\qquad
\sinh\eta\cosh\eta=\eta+\frac{2}{3}\eta^{3}+O(\eta^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that for the long wave thin plate, $\eta=kh\ll1$,
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{{\rm imp}}(\eta)=\frac{\eta}{4}+O(\eta^{3}),\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}  .
\end{equation*}
Absorbing the explicit $kh$ factor into the definition of the basal
traction this is the familiar $O(1)$ dynamic topography law
$e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}=\frac14 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.  This is the $kh/4$ law
commonly quoted in the literature. 

Remark.  If the vertical velocity at the base is not imposed,
$\Phi(h)\ne0$, the four dynamical conditions $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ close the problem
without $\Phi(h)=0$.  The steady solution can then be written in
closed form
\begin{equation*}
